#### Problem 1: Implicit Trust Within Perimeter

Traditional network security assumes services within the same VNET or behind the firewall are trustworthy. Gainwell's microservices communicate via HTTP without authentication, relying on network isolation for security.

**Technical Manifestation:**

```csharp
// VULNERABLE: Internal microservice calling another service without authentication
public class ClaimsService
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    public async Task<MemberInfo> GetMemberInfo(string memberId)
    {
        // Direct HTTP call to Member Service (same VNET)
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"http://member-service:8080/members/{memberId}");

        // NO AUTHENTICATION: Assumes caller is trusted because it's internal
        // NO AUTHORIZATION: No verification of caller's right to access this member
        // NO AUDIT: No record of which service accessed member data

        return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<MemberInfo>();
    }
}
```

**Impact:**
- If one microservice is compromised (code vulnerability, dependency exploit), attacker gains access to all internal services
- No visibility into which services access sensitive data (audit gap)
- Difficult to enforce least-privilege: all internal services have equal access

