### Example 1: Provider Portal Access with Conditional Access

**Context:** Healthcare providers access Gainwell Provider+™ portal to submit claims and check patient eligibility.

**Zero Trust Implementation:**

```csharp
// Provider Portal: Risk-Based Authentication
public class ProviderAuthenticationService
{
    public async Task<AuthResult> AuthenticateProvider(ProviderLoginRequest request)
    {
        // 1. Initial authentication
        var authResult = await _azureAD.Authenticate(request.Username, request.Password);

        // 2. Evaluate Conditional Access policy
        var conditionsmet = new ConditionalAccessContext
        {
            UserId = authResult.UserId,
            DeviceId = request.DeviceId,
            Location = request.IPAddress,
            Application = "provider-portal",
            RiskLevel = await _identityProtection.GetUserRiskLevel(authResult.UserId)
        };

        var policyResult = await _azureAD.EvaluateConditionalAccess(conditionsmet);

        // 3. Enforce MFA if required
        if (policyResult.RequiresMFA && !authResult.MFACompleted)
        {
            return AuthResult.RequireMFA();
        }

        // 4. Check device compliance
        var deviceCompliant = await _intune.IsDeviceCompliant(request.DeviceId);
        if (!deviceCompliant)
        {
            await _audit.Log("Device non-compliant", authResult.UserId, request.DeviceId);
            return AuthResult.Denied("Device does not meet compliance requirements");
        }

        // 5. Issue short-lived token
        var token = await _tokenService.IssueToken(authResult.UserId,
            expiresIn: TimeSpan.FromMinutes(15));

        return AuthResult.Success(token);
    }
}
```

**Outcome:** Conditional access enforces device compliance and risk-based MFA on every authentication attempt, ensuring that stolen credentials alone are insufficient for unauthorized access.

